    narrationText: `Standard Precautions are the foundation of infection control and must be observed by every healthcare worker for every patient. This is a critical concept: standard precautions are based on the degree of exposure, not the patient's diagnosis. You must assume that blood and all body fluids from all patients are potentially infectious. Your use of personal protective equipment must focus on your interaction with the patient's blood or body fluids at the time of treatment, not on what condition the patient has. Standard precautions prevent reasonably anticipated exposure to blood and other potentially infectious body fluids through the skin, eyes, mucous membranes, or parenteral routes. The key components of standard precautions include hand hygiene before and after every patient contact, the use of gloves when contact with blood, body fluids, mucous membranes, or non-intact skin is anticipated, the use of gowns or aprons when splashing or spraying is possible, the use of goggles or protective glasses when splash to the eyes is possible, the use of masks when respiratory precautions are indicated, proper sharps handling, proper cleaning and disinfection of equipment, proper handling of contaminated linens, and restrictions on eating, drinking, or smoking in patient care areas. Healthcare workers with open wounds or weeping skin conditions should refrain from direct patient care until the condition resolves. The agency maintains a log for collection, transportation, and disposition of hazardous waste.`,
