    narrationText: `Hand hygiene is the single most important measure for preventing the spread of infection. Your agency policy requires thorough hand washing or hand antisepsis for all employees. Alcohol-based hand sanitizer is the preferred method for routine hand decontamination ΓÇö use it until your hands are dry. If antimicrobial hand soap is used, wash for at least fifteen seconds. You must perform hand hygiene in the following situations: before and after caring for each patient, when coming in contact with objects or equipment near the patient, between tasks on the same patient especially during prolonged contact like bathing, after removing gloves, after touching potentially contaminated objects, after caring for a patient with drug-resistant organisms like C-diff or MRSA, after using the toilet or blowing your nose, after assisting a patient with toileting, before eating or handling food, and before donning sterile gloves for invasive procedures. Important exceptions: when hands are visibly dirty or soiled with blood or body fluids, soap and water must be used first ΓÇö alcohol sanitizer alone is not sufficient. For C-difficile specifically, the CDC recommends soap and water hand washing, not alcohol-based sanitizer, because C-diff spores are resistant to alcohol. Additional hand hygiene requirements include keeping natural nail tips less than one quarter inch long, not wearing artificial fingernails when providing direct patient care, wearing gloves when there is risk of contact with blood or body fluids, and rings are discouraged except for a plain wedding band. Paper towels should be used to dry hands and to turn off the faucet after washing.`,
