  {
    pageId: 'M05-P05', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M05', pageType: 'instruction',
    title: 'Bag Technique ΓÇö Preventing Cross-Contamination',
    narrationText: `The bag technique is essential for preventing cross-contamination between patient homes. Your nursing bag is a potential vehicle for carrying organisms from one home to another if not managed properly. Key bag technique rules: floors are considered highly contaminated. Bags must never be placed on the floor. Keep the bag zipped and stored in a clean protected area in both the home and your car. When carrying the bag into a home, keep it on your shoulder until you can place a barrier on a table or hard chair. The barrier is discarded after use. When a patient has a virulent organism infection or vermin are present, place the supplies you need in two plastic bags before entering. After providing care, pull the inner bag out of the outer bag. The outer bag becomes contaminated and is discarded in the home. Cleanse your hands before entering or removing supplies from the bag and before examining the patient. Keep the bag orderly with minimum necessary items so supplies can be found quickly without rummaging. If additional supplies are needed during the visit, cleanse hands before re-entering the bag. All equipment must be cleansed with disinfectant after use and before returning to the bag ΓÇö stethoscope bells, scissors, thermometers with covers, and measuring tapes. Once a week, wipe the outside of the bag with disinfectant. Once a month, remove all supplies, thoroughly cleanse the bag, dry it, and return clean equipment. When breaks in bag technique occur, staff will be instructed by the Director and must demonstrate correct technique. A joint visit is required, with follow-up in six months.`,
