  { pageId: 'M06-P02', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Types of Communication Barriers',
    narrationText: `There are seven major types of communication barriers you will encounter. Physical barriers separate people and include closed doors and distance. Language barriers occur when words, expressions, or jargon are not understood ΓÇö this applies to different languages and medical terminology patients may not know. Gender barriers arise from different communication styles between masculine and feminine approaches. Interpersonal barriers are created when people distance themselves through withdrawal or superficial interactions. Perceptual barriers result from different worldviews ΓÇö viewing a message only from your own perspective rather than considering the other person's viewpoint. Cultural barriers stem from ethnic, religious, and social differences. Emotional barriers occur when hostility, anger, fear, or other strong emotions prevent effective listening. Non-verbal communication is equally important. Research suggests that only seven percent of communication comes from the actual words we say. Non-verbal components include kinesics, which is facial expressions and gestures; oculesics, which is eye contact; haptics, which is the role of touch; proxemics, which is personal space; chronemics, which is the use of time; paralinguistics, which is tone, pitch, and pacing; and physical appearance. Understanding these barriers is the first step to overcoming them in the field.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>7 Communication Barriers</h3><ol><li>Physical ΓÇö distance, closed doors</li><li>Language ΓÇö different languages, jargon</li><li>Gender ΓÇö different communication styles</li><li>Interpersonal ΓÇö withdrawal, superficial contact</li><li>Perceptual ΓÇö different worldviews</li><li>Cultural ΓÇö ethnic, religious, social differences</li><li>Emotional ΓÇö hostility, anger, fear</li></ol><h3>Non-Verbal Communication (93% of message)</h3><p>Kinesics, oculesics, haptics, proxemics, chronemics, paralinguistics, physical appearance</p>`,
    media: createMedia('Seven barrier types as illustrated icons in a circle. Non-verbal components listed below.', '10-second 720p: Seven barriers appear with icons. "93% of communication is non-verbal." Professional.', 'image', 'duringNarration', 'Barrier types infographic.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P02', 'instruction', 'Types of Communication Barriers', `Seven barrier types and non-verbal communication components.`, 2),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C01', lessonId: 'M06-P02', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Identifying Barriers', scenario: 'Your patient, an elderly Vietnamese woman, nods and smiles during your teaching but her grandson later tells you she did not understand anything you said because she speaks very little English.', narrationText: 'A patient appeared to understand but actually did not due to a language barrier. What barriers were at play?', prompt: 'What communication barriers contributed to this situation?', interactionType: 'identify-the-risk', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Language barrier (limited English) and cultural barrier (nodding as politeness rather than understanding). The teach-back method would have identified the comprehension gap. A qualified interpreter should have been used.', 'Correct. The language barrier prevented understanding, while the cultural norm of nodding to show respect masked the comprehension gap. Teach-back and interpreter services are the solutions.', true), createChallengeOption('b', 'Only a language barrier.', 'Language is the primary barrier, but the cultural practice of nodding as politeness also contributed by masking the lack of understanding.', false), createChallengeOption('c', 'No barriers ΓÇö the patient chose not to listen.', 'The patient could not understand due to language limitations. This is not a choice.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Language + cultural barriers. Use interpreter and teach-back.', teachingPoint: 'Nodding does not equal understanding. Always use teach-back and qualified interpreters for LEP patients.', documentationPrompt: 'Document language barrier identified, interpreter needed, teach-back to be used for all future teaching.', escalationPrompt: 'Arrange interpreter services for all future visits with this patient.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 4, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P03', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Language Access and Interpreter Services',
    narrationText: `Your agency has a clear policy on language access. When a staff member does not speak the patient's language, the agency provides qualified interpreter services at no charge to the patient. Children and other patients will not be used to interpret, to ensure confidentiality and accurate communication. The patient may prefer to use a family member, friend, or significant other, but qualified interpreters must be available when no one in the home can provide adequate interpretation. Your agency contracts with interpreter services including Accredited Language Services at 1-800-322-0284, Verbatim Solutions at 1-800-575-5702, and Language Line. For patients who are deaf or hard of hearing, the agency will use the state relay system and make every effort to obtain sign language interpreter services. Educational materials, visual aids, and special devices will be used as needed. Written and verbal communication must be at an educational level the patient understands. When a significant portion of the caseload does not speak English, written materials must be provided in a language understandable to patients. Remember: federal laws require that no one be denied or forced to wait for medical care due to language barriers. Reliance on untrained interpreters has been shown to have negative clinical consequences. Always use qualified services.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Agency Interpreter Policy</h3><ul><li>Qualified interpreter at no charge to patient</li><li>Children NOT used as interpreters</li><li>Patient may prefer family/friend but qualified service must be available</li></ul><h3>Interpreter Services</h3><ul><li>Accredited Language Services: 1-800-322-0284</li><li>Verbatim Solutions: 1-800-575-5702</li><li>Language Line: www.languageline.com</li><li>Deaf/hard of hearing: state relay system + sign language interpreters</li></ul><h3>Materials</h3><ul><li>At patient's educational/literacy level</li><li>In patient's language when significant LEP caseload</li></ul>`,
    media: createMedia('Split image: phone interpreter service and in-person sign language interpreter. Agency policy card visible.', '10-second 720p: Nurse uses phone interpreter, then sign language interpreter shown. Text: "Qualified Interpreters ΓÇö Every Visit."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Interpreter services illustration.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P03', 'instruction', 'Language Access', `Agency provides qualified interpreters at no charge. Children not used. Federal law prohibits care denial due to language.`, 2),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C02', lessonId: 'M06-P03', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Interpreter Decision', scenario: 'You arrive at a Spanish-speaking patient\'s home. Her 10-year-old grandson offers to translate. You need to discuss medication changes including side effects.', narrationText: 'A child offers to translate for a medication discussion. What should you do?', prompt: 'Should you use the child as interpreter?', interactionType: 'choose-best-response', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Accept the grandson\'s help since he speaks both languages.', 'Agency policy prohibits using children as interpreters. Children lack medical vocabulary and using them compromises confidentiality.', false), createChallengeOption('b', 'Politely decline, explain that agency policy requires qualified interpreters for medical discussions. Call the agency interpreter service (Accredited Language Services or Verbatim Solutions) to connect a Spanish interpreter by phone. Then discuss medication changes through the qualified interpreter using teach-back to verify understanding.', 'Correct. Children cannot serve as medical interpreters. Qualified phone interpreter services ensure accurate, confidential communication.', true), createChallengeOption('c', 'Use hand gestures and the medication bottle to explain.', 'Gestures are insufficient for discussing medication changes and side effects. Miscommunication could be dangerous.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Decline child interpreter. Use qualified phone service. Teach-back through interpreter.', teachingPoint: 'Agency policy: children are not used as interpreters. Qualified interpreter services ensure accuracy and confidentiality.', documentationPrompt: 'Document: language barrier, interpreter service used, teaching provided through interpreter, teach-back completed.', escalationPrompt: 'If interpreter service is unavailable, notify supervisor to arrange alternative.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 4, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P04', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Communicating with Deaf and Hard-of-Hearing Patients',
    narrationText: `More than twenty-two million Americans have some form of hearing loss. Deaf people communicate in various ways depending on when deafness began, type and degree of hearing loss, language skills, and personal preference. Some use speech, sign language, fingerspelling, lip reading, writing, or combinations. Key tips: get the person's attention before speaking with a tap, wave, or visual signal. Speak slowly and clearly but do not yell or exaggerate ΓÇö distorted lip movements make speechreading harder. Maintain eye contact and speak directly to the deaf person, not to the interpreter. Do not stand in front of a light source which creates glare. If not understood, rephrase rather than repeating the same words. Use open-ended questions, not yes-or-no questions, to verify understanding. Use visual aids, drawings, and written communication as supplements. Be courteous and do not ignore the deaf person if the phone rings. The Americans with Disabilities Act guarantees workplace accommodations. Consider sensory sensitivity ΓÇö many deaf people have residual hearing and are bothered by loud noises. Use visual signals for emergency alerts. Your agency will use the state relay system for telephone communication with deaf patients.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Communicating with Deaf/HOH Patients</h3><ul><li>Get attention: tap, wave, visual signal</li><li>Speak slowly, clearly ΓÇö no yelling, no exaggeration</li><li>Maintain eye contact, speak to the person not the interpreter</li><li>No backlit position (avoid standing in front of windows)</li><li>Rephrase if not understood, don't just repeat</li><li>Open-ended questions to verify understanding</li><li>Visual aids, writing, drawings as supplements</li><li>State relay system for phone communication</li></ul>`,
    media: createMedia('Illustration of nurse communicating with deaf patient. Nurse faces patient directly, good lighting, visual aids on table.', '10-second 720p: Nurse faces deaf patient, maintains eye contact, uses visual aid. Text: "Face-to-Face ┬╖ Clear ┬╖ Visual."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Deaf communication illustration.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P04', 'instruction', 'Deaf/HOH Communication', `Tips for communicating with deaf patients: get attention, speak clearly, maintain eye contact, use visual aids, open-ended questions.`, 2),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C03', lessonId: 'M06-P04', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'HOH Patient Communication', scenario: 'Your patient, Mr. Rogers, wears hearing aids but still has difficulty understanding you. He keeps nodding but gives inconsistent answers to your questions.', narrationText: 'Mr. Rogers nods but gives inconsistent answers. How do you improve communication?', prompt: 'What adjustments should you make?', interactionType: 'choose-best-response', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Speak louder.', 'Simply speaking louder may distort sound through hearing aids and does not address comprehension. Other strategies are needed.', false), createChallengeOption('b', 'Face Mr. Rogers directly with good lighting. Reduce background noise. Speak clearly at a normal pace. Use written notes and visual aids to supplement verbal communication. Use open-ended questions to verify understanding. Ask him about his preferred communication method.', 'Correct. Multiple strategies work together: positioning, lighting, noise reduction, visual supplements, open-ended questions, and asking about preferences.', true), createChallengeOption('c', 'Write everything down and stop talking.', 'Written communication alone may be insufficient and can feel impersonal. A combination of verbal and written methods is best.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Face patient, good lighting, reduce noise, visual aids, open-ended questions, ask preferences.', teachingPoint: 'Inconsistent answers signal comprehension issues. Use multiple strategies and ask the patient what works best for them.', documentationPrompt: 'Document communication barrier, strategies used, patient\'s preferred method, and teaching effectiveness.', escalationPrompt: 'If communication prevents safe care, request ASL interpreter or assistive device support.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 4, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P05', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Health Literacy and Teach-Back',
    narrationText: `Health literacy refers to the ability of patients to understand health information and make informed decisions about their care. Low health literacy is common and cuts across all demographics. Patients with low health literacy may have difficulty reading medication labels, understanding consent forms, following care instructions, and recognizing when to seek emergency care. As a field worker, you must assess health literacy informally by observing the patient's responses to written and verbal information. Simplify your language ΓÇö avoid medical jargon. Use pictures and demonstrations when possible. The teach-back method is essential: after providing instruction, ask the patient to explain back to you in their own words what they understood. This tests your communication, not the patient's intelligence. Written and verbal communication must be at an educational level the patient understands. Educational materials should be appropriate in content and vocabulary to the patient's educational level, literacy, and language. When a significant portion of your caseload does not speak English, written materials must be provided in their language. Visual aids and special devices should be used to facilitate communication when standard methods are insufficient.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Health Literacy</h3><ul><li>Ability to understand health info and make informed decisions</li><li>Low literacy is common across all demographics</li><li>Signs: difficulty with labels, forms, instructions, recognizing emergencies</li></ul><h3>Your Strategies</h3><ul><li>Simplify language ΓÇö no jargon</li><li>Use pictures and demonstrations</li><li>Teach-back: patient explains back in own words</li><li>Materials at patient's educational/literacy/language level</li><li>Visual aids and special devices as needed</li></ul>`,
    media: createMedia('Nurse using picture-based medication schedule with patient. Simple, clear visual aid visible.', '10-second 720p: Picture medication schedule shown. Patient points to images. Text: "Simplify ┬╖ Visualize ┬╖ Verify."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Health literacy aids.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P05', 'instruction', 'Health Literacy', `Health literacy affects all demographics. Simplify language, use visuals, teach-back method, appropriate materials.`, 2),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C04', lessonId: 'M06-P05', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Low Literacy Teaching', scenario: 'You are teaching a patient about wound care. She cannot read the written instructions you provided. She looks embarrassed and pushes the paper away.', narrationText: 'Your patient cannot read the wound care instructions and appears embarrassed. How do you adapt?', prompt: 'What is the best teaching approach?', interactionType: 'teach-back-practice', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Read the instructions aloud to her.', 'Reading aloud helps but does not address the embarrassment or provide a take-home resource she can use independently.', false), createChallengeOption('b', 'Acknowledge without judgment. Demonstrate the wound care procedure step by step. Use picture-based instructions that show each step visually. Have her practice while you observe. Use teach-back to confirm understanding. Leave the picture-based instructions for reference. Document literacy level in the chart so future visitors know to use visual methods.', 'Correct. Non-judgmental adaptation, demonstration, picture-based materials, practice, teach-back, and documentation ensure safe care regardless of literacy level.', true), createChallengeOption('c', 'Skip the teaching and just do the wound care yourself each visit.', 'Not empowering the patient with knowledge reduces their ability to participate in their own care and recognize problems between visits.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Non-judgmental, demonstrate, picture instructions, practice, teach-back, document literacy level.', teachingPoint: 'Adapt teaching methods to the patient without making them feel ashamed. Visual and demonstration methods work for all literacy levels.', documentationPrompt: 'Document literacy barrier, teaching methods adapted (visual, demonstration), teach-back result, materials left in home.', escalationPrompt: 'Notify office to provide picture-based materials for this patient. Update care plan for visual teaching methods.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 5, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  // M06 Pages 6-10: Scenario challenges, documentation practice, escalation (following same pattern)
  { pageId: 'M06-P06', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Cognitive Barriers and Caregiver Involvement',
    narrationText: `Cognitive barriers affect patients with dementia, intellectual disabilities, brain injuries, or medication-related confusion. These patients may have difficulty processing information, remembering instructions, making decisions, or communicating their needs. Strategies include speaking in simple, short sentences, using one instruction at a time, allowing extra time for processing, repeating information patiently, involving the primary caregiver in all teaching, using consistent routines, and documenting the patient's communication abilities and preferences so all team members use the same approach. Caregiver involvement is essential when the patient cannot independently manage their care. Teach the caregiver using the same principles: appropriate language level, demonstrations, teach-back, and written or visual aids. Document who was taught and their demonstrated understanding. When communication barriers prevent safe care ΓÇö when you cannot reliably confirm the patient understands critical instructions ΓÇö escalate to your supervisor. This may require additional resources such as cognitive assessments, interpreter services, assistive devices, or changes to the care plan.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Cognitive Barriers</h3><ul><li>Dementia, brain injury, medication confusion</li><li>Simple sentences, one instruction at a time</li><li>Extra processing time, patient repetition</li><li>Consistent routines across all team members</li></ul><h3>Caregiver Teaching</h3><ul><li>Same principles: appropriate language, demonstration, teach-back</li><li>Document who was taught and understanding</li></ul><h3>Escalation</h3><p>When communication prevents safe care ΓåÆ notify supervisor for additional resources</p>`,
    media: createMedia('Nurse teaching both patient and caregiver together. Simple visual aid being shared.', '10-second 720p: Nurse, patient, and caregiver at table. Simple visual instruction. Text: "Include the Caregiver."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Caregiver teaching scene.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P06', 'instruction', 'Cognitive Barriers', `Cognitive barrier strategies and caregiver involvement. Escalate when communication prevents safe care.`, 2),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C05', lessonId: 'M06-P06', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Cognitive Barrier Scenario', scenario: 'Your patient with early dementia keeps forgetting to take afternoon medications. His wife works during the day.', narrationText: 'A dementia patient forgets afternoon medications. His caregiver works during the day. How do you adapt?', prompt: 'What strategies address this medication safety concern?', interactionType: 'scenario-decision', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Set up a visual medication reminder system (picture-based chart, pill organizer with day/time labels, alarm clock set for medication time). Teach the patient using consistent language and routine. Teach the wife the backup monitoring plan for evenings. Document the cognitive barrier and system in the care plan. Consider recommending a medication management evaluation to the physician.', 'Correct. Multiple strategies address the barrier: visual systems, consistent routines, caregiver backup, documentation, and physician involvement if needed.', true), createChallengeOption('b', 'Tell the patient to try harder to remember.', 'Dementia prevents the patient from simply trying harder. Adaptive systems are needed.', false), createChallengeOption('c', 'Stop prescribing afternoon medications.', 'Medication changes are physician decisions, not field worker decisions. Provide adaptive support while recommending evaluation.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Visual systems, consistent routine, caregiver backup plan, documentation, physician recommendation.', teachingPoint: 'Cognitive barriers require adaptive systems, not just repeated instruction. Involve caregivers and recommend clinical evaluation when safety is concerned.', documentationPrompt: 'Document cognitive barrier, medication management system implemented, caregiver teaching, physician recommendation.', escalationPrompt: 'Notify physician about medication management concern and recommend cognitive evaluation if not already done.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 5, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P07', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'scenario-challenge', title: 'Comprehensive Communication Scenario',
    narrationText: `You visit Mrs. Okafor, a sixty-eight-year-old Nigerian-born patient who speaks Igbo as her primary language and limited English. She has diabetes and needs insulin teaching. She has a vision impairment and cannot read small print. Her adult daughter is present but speaks only slightly more English than her mother. You need to teach insulin injection technique, blood sugar monitoring, and signs of hypoglycemia. How do you approach this visit with multiple communication barriers?`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Multi-Barrier Scenario</h3><p>Patient: Mrs. Okafor, Igbo-speaking, limited English, vision impairment, diabetes</p><p>Task: Insulin teaching, blood sugar monitoring, hypoglycemia signs</p><p>Barriers: Language, vision, health literacy</p>`,
    media: createMedia('Home visit scene with multiple communication aids visible.', '10-second 720p: Multiple barriers shown. "Multiple Barriers ΓÇö One Plan."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Multi-barrier scenario.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P07', 'scenario-challenge', 'Multi-Barrier Scenario', `Multiple communication barriers requiring comprehensive approach.`, 5),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C06', lessonId: 'M06-P07', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Multi-Barrier Response', scenario: 'Language (Igbo), vision impairment, health literacy ΓÇö teach insulin management.', narrationText: 'Address all three barriers in your teaching plan.', prompt: 'What is the comprehensive communication approach?', interactionType: 'scenario-decision', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Connect qualified Igbo interpreter by phone. Use large-print visual aids with pictures for each insulin step (accommodating vision). Demonstrate injection technique on practice pad. Have Mrs. Okafor practice while you observe through the interpreter. Use teach-back through interpreter to verify understanding of injection, monitoring, and hypoglycemia signs. Provide large-print picture-based materials in Igbo if available, or simple English with pictures. Document all barriers, interpreter used, teaching methods, and demonstrated competency.', 'Correct. Every barrier is addressed: interpreter for language, large-print visuals for vision, demonstration and practice for health literacy, teach-back through interpreter for verification.', true), createChallengeOption('b', 'Use the daughter to translate and give standard written materials.', 'The daughter\'s limited English makes her an inadequate interpreter. Standard materials do not accommodate vision or language barriers.', false), createChallengeOption('c', 'Demonstrate without verbal explanation since actions speak louder than words.', 'Silent demonstration without verbal teaching through an interpreter is insufficient for complex medication management.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Qualified interpreter + large-print visuals + demonstration + practice + teach-back + appropriate materials.', teachingPoint: 'Multiple barriers require multiple solutions working together. Never rely on a single strategy when several barriers are present.', documentationPrompt: 'Document each barrier, strategy used, interpreter service, teaching methods, patient demonstration, teach-back results, materials provided.', escalationPrompt: 'Request Igbo-language materials from the office if available. Arrange interpreter for all future visits.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 5, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P08', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'documentation-practice', title: 'Communication Barrier Documentation',
    narrationText: `Documentation of communication barriers ensures continuity. Every team member who visits the patient should know what barriers exist and what strategies work. Document the specific barrier identified, the accommodation method used, interpreter service details, teaching methods adapted, teach-back results, caregiver involvement and their demonstrated understanding, materials provided and in what language or format, and any escalation needed. When communication prevents safe care and no accommodation can bridge the gap, document your assessment and escalate immediately to your supervisor.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Documentation Checklist</h3><ul><li>Barrier identified (specific type)</li><li>Accommodation method used</li><li>Interpreter details (service, language, method)</li><li>Teaching methods adapted</li><li>Teach-back results</li><li>Caregiver involvement and understanding</li><li>Materials provided (language/format)</li><li>Escalation if needed</li></ul>`,
    media: createMedia('Documentation template with communication barrier fields.', '10-second 720p: Fields populate. "Document Every Barrier & Strategy."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Documentation template.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P08', 'documentation-practice', 'Documentation', `Document barriers, accommodations, interpreters, teaching, teach-back, and escalation.`, 6),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C07', lessonId: 'M06-P08', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Documentation Practice', scenario: 'Document your visit with Mrs. Okafor.', narrationText: 'Select the most complete documentation entry.', prompt: 'Best documentation?', interactionType: 'documentation-practice', options: [createChallengeOption('a', '"Interpreter used. Teaching done."', 'Missing barrier details, specific accommodations, teach-back results, and materials.', false), createChallengeOption('b', '"Barriers: (1) Language ΓÇö Igbo-speaking, limited English. Igbo interpreter via Accredited Language Services connected for visit. (2) Vision ΓÇö cannot read standard print. Large-print materials with pictures used. (3) Health literacy ΓÇö demonstration + practice approach used. Teaching: Insulin injection technique demonstrated, patient practiced on pad under observation ΓÇö technique correct after 2 attempts. Blood sugar monitoring demonstrated, patient able to return-demonstrate with verbal cues through interpreter. Hypoglycemia signs taught ΓÇö patient verbalized 3 of 4 signs through interpreter (missed \'shakiness\', reteaching provided). Caregiver (daughter) present, taught backup monitoring. Large-print picture guide left in home. Follow-up: reinforce hypoglycemia signs next visit. Interpreter needed for all visits."', 'Excellent. Every barrier documented with specific accommodations, detailed teaching outcomes, teach-back specifics, and follow-up plan.', true)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Comprehensive barrier documentation with specific accommodation details and outcomes.', teachingPoint: 'Detailed communication barrier documentation ensures every team member can provide effective care.', documentationPrompt: 'Use this level of detail for every visit with significant communication barriers.', escalationPrompt: 'Update care plan to reflect interpreter need and visual accommodation for all future visits.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 5, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P09', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'documentation-practice', title: 'Escalation When Communication Prevents Safe Care',
    narrationText: `There are situations where communication barriers are so severe that safe care cannot be provided. When you cannot verify that a patient understands critical safety instructions ΓÇö medication dosing, emergency signs, equipment operation ΓÇö despite using interpreters, visual aids, and teach-back, you must escalate. Report to your supervisor with specific documentation of the barriers encountered, the strategies attempted, and why safe care cannot be confirmed. The supervisor may arrange additional resources such as more frequent visits, bilingual staff assignment, assistive technology, cognitive assessment referral, or care plan modification. Never continue providing care when you believe the communication gap creates a safety risk without notifying your supervisor. Your professional judgment that communication prevents safe care must be documented and acted upon.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Escalation Triggers</h3><ul><li>Cannot verify patient understands critical safety instructions</li><li>Interpreter services insufficient for complexity</li><li>Cognitive barriers prevent comprehension despite all adaptations</li><li>No caregiver available to bridge the gap</li></ul><h3>Escalation Process</h3><ol><li>Document barriers, strategies tried, and why care is unsafe</li><li>Notify supervisor immediately</li><li>Supervisor arranges: more visits, bilingual staff, assistive tech, assessments, care plan changes</li></ol>`,
    media: createMedia('Escalation flowchart: Barrier ΓåÆ Strategies ΓåÆ Still Unsafe ΓåÆ Escalate to Supervisor.', '10-second 720p: Escalation path shown. "When Barriers Prevent Safe Care ΓÇö Escalate."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Escalation flowchart.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P09', 'documentation-practice', 'Escalation', `Escalate when communication prevents safe care despite all attempts.`, 4),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C08', lessonId: 'M06-P09', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Escalation Decision', scenario: 'Your patient speaks a rare dialect not covered by interpreter services. He lives alone, has no caregiver, and cannot demonstrate understanding of his new anticoagulant medication despite multiple teaching attempts with visual aids.', narrationText: 'No interpreter available for a rare dialect. Patient alone. Cannot confirm understanding of high-risk medication. What do you do?', prompt: 'How do you handle this critical communication gap?', interactionType: 'escalation-decision', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Leave the medication and hope for the best.', 'Leaving a high-risk medication without confirmed understanding is dangerous and violates your duty to ensure safe care.', false), createChallengeOption('b', 'Escalate to your supervisor immediately. Document the specific barrier (dialect not available through interpreter services), all strategies attempted, and that you cannot confirm safe understanding of anticoagulant instructions. Do not leave the medication unsupervised without confirmed understanding. The supervisor must coordinate a solution ΓÇö which may include finding a community interpreter, arranging a bilingual home health aide, adjusting the medication regimen with the physician, or arranging supervised medication administration.', 'Correct. This is a patient safety issue requiring immediate escalation. High-risk medications without confirmed understanding create dangerous situations.', true), createChallengeOption('c', 'Teach using only hand gestures and leave written instructions in English.', 'Hand gestures and English instructions are insufficient for anticoagulant management where dosing errors can cause serious harm.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Immediate escalation. Do not leave high-risk medication without confirmed understanding. Supervisor coordinates solution.', teachingPoint: 'When communication barriers create medication safety risks, escalation is not optional ΓÇö it is required.', documentationPrompt: 'Document: specific barrier, all strategies attempted, inability to confirm understanding, escalation to supervisor with date/time, medication status.', escalationPrompt: 'Call supervisor NOW. This is a patient safety issue requiring immediate coordination.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 4, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P10', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'instruction', title: 'Written Communication and Cultural Considerations',
    narrationText: `Written communication in healthcare must be clear, at the appropriate reading level, and culturally sensitive. Effective written materials use simple language, short sentences, pictures, and clear formatting. Avoid medical jargon. Your agency policy requires that cultural considerations be respected and observed for all patients. Where cultural considerations impede prescribed care, notify your supervisor and physician. Different cultures may have different views on: eye contact, personal space, touch, family decision-making structures, gender roles in healthcare, pain expression, and end-of-life decisions. You are not expected to know every cultural norm, but you are expected to ask respectfully, listen carefully, adapt your communication approach, and document cultural preferences so the entire team can provide consistent, respectful care.`,
    contentHtml: `<h3>Written Communication Standards</h3><ul><li>Simple language, short sentences, pictures</li><li>No medical jargon</li><li>Appropriate reading level</li><li>Available in patient's language when needed</li></ul><h3>Cultural Communication Awareness</h3><ul><li>Eye contact norms vary</li><li>Personal space preferences differ</li><li>Touch may be restricted</li><li>Family decision-making structures vary</li><li>Ask respectfully, listen, adapt, document</li></ul>`,
    media: createMedia('Simple patient education material with pictures alongside text in two languages.', '10-second 720p: Bilingual materials shown. Simple, clear design. "Communicate at Their Level."', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Multilingual materials.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P10', 'instruction', 'Written Communication', `Written materials must be clear, simple, and culturally appropriate. Ask, listen, adapt, document.`, 4),
    challenge: { challengeId: 'M06-C09', lessonId: 'M06-P10', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', title: 'Cultural Communication Adaptation', scenario: 'Your Hmong patient\'s family insists that the eldest son must be present for all care decisions, even though the patient is a competent adult woman who can speak for herself.', narrationText: 'A family insists the eldest son be present for all decisions. How do you balance cultural respect with patient autonomy?', prompt: 'What is the best approach?', interactionType: 'scenario-decision', options: [createChallengeOption('a', 'Refuse the family\'s request and insist on speaking to the patient alone.', 'Refusing a cultural norm without first understanding it and finding a balance may alienate the family and the patient.', false), createChallengeOption('b', 'Respect the cultural preference for family involvement while also ensuring the patient has the opportunity to express her own wishes. If possible, speak with the patient privately at some point during the visit to confirm her preferences. Include the son in discussions as the family requests. Document the cultural communication preference in the care plan.', 'Correct. Balance cultural respect with patient autonomy. Include family as culturally preferred while ensuring the patient can express her own wishes.', true), createChallengeOption('c', 'Only talk to the son and ignore the patient.', 'The patient is competent and has rights. Ignoring her violates patient rights even if the family has cultural preferences.', false)], bestPracticeAnswer: 'Respect cultural norm AND ensure patient autonomy. Include family, verify patient preferences, document.', teachingPoint: 'Cultural family involvement and patient autonomy are not mutually exclusive. Find the balance and document.', documentationPrompt: 'Document cultural communication preference, family involvement, and confirmation of patient\'s own wishes.', escalationPrompt: 'If cultural practices prevent you from confirming patient consent, escalate to supervisor.', estimatedInteractionMinutes: 4, requiredForProgression: true, graded: false, countsTowardFinalScore: false, reviewRequired: false },
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  // M06 Pre-Assessment, Final, Remediation, Attestation, Certificate (same structure)
  { pageId: 'M06-P11', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'pre-assessment', title: 'Pre-Assessment: Communication Barriers',
    narrationText: `Six-question pre-assessment. Not graded.`, contentHtml: `<h3>Pre-Assessment</h3><p>6 questions</p>`,
    media: createMedia('Clipboard.', '10-second 720p: Checklist.', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Pre-assessment.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P11', 'pre-assessment', 'Pre-Assessment', `Six questions.`, 8),
    assessmentQuestions: [
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q01', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Communication barriers include:', options: [createOption('a', 'Physical barriers only', false, 'Barriers include physical, language, gender, interpersonal, perceptual, cultural, and emotional.'), createOption('b', 'Physical, language, gender, interpersonal, perceptual, cultural, and emotional barriers', true, 'Correct. All seven types are communication barriers.'), createOption('c', 'Only language barriers', false, 'Language is one of seven types.'), createOption('d', 'Only cultural barriers', false, 'Cultural is one of seven types.')], mappedObjective: 'Identify barrier types', source: 'provided', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q02', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Children should NOT be used as interpreters because:', options: [createOption('a', 'They are too young to understand', false, 'The issue is confidentiality and accuracy, not age alone.'), createOption('b', 'To ensure confidentiality of information and accurate communication', true, 'Correct. Children lack medical vocabulary and using them compromises confidentiality.'), createOption('c', 'It is illegal', false, 'It is agency policy, not necessarily illegal.'), createOption('d', 'They charge too much', false, 'Children do not charge.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply interpreter policy', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q03', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Non-verbal communication accounts for approximately what percentage of the message?', options: [createOption('a', '7%', false, 'Seven percent is the VERBAL (word) component.'), createOption('b', '50%', false, 'Non-verbal accounts for more than 50%.'), createOption('c', '93%', true, 'Correct. Research suggests 93% of communication is non-verbal.'), createOption('d', '25%', false, 'Non-verbal is much higher.')], mappedObjective: 'Understand non-verbal communication', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q04', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'When communicating with a deaf person, you should:', options: [createOption('a', 'Yell so they can hear better', false, 'Yelling distorts lip movements and does not help.'), createOption('b', 'Maintain eye contact, speak clearly, use open-ended questions', true, 'Correct. Face the person, maintain eye contact, speak clearly, and use open-ended questions.'), createOption('c', 'Always write everything down', false, 'Written communication is one option but not always preferred. Ask the person.'), createOption('d', 'Avoid eye contact to not make them uncomfortable', false, 'Eye contact is essential for deaf communication.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply deaf communication techniques', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q05', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Teach-back tests:', options: [createOption('a', 'The patient\'s intelligence', false, 'Teach-back tests YOUR communication, not the patient.'), createOption('b', 'The effectiveness of your communication', true, 'Correct. Teach-back verifies that you communicated effectively.'), createOption('c', 'The patient\'s memory', false, 'It is not a memory test.'), createOption('d', 'The patient\'s reading ability', false, 'It is not a reading test.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply teach-back', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-PRE-Q06', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'When communication barriers prevent safe care, you should:', options: [createOption('a', 'Continue providing care as best you can', false, 'Unsafe care should not continue without escalation.'), createOption('b', 'Escalate to your supervisor immediately', true, 'Correct. When barriers prevent safe care, immediate escalation is required.'), createOption('c', 'Discharge the patient', false, 'Discharge is not your decision.'), createOption('d', 'Ask the patient to find their own interpreter', false, 'The agency provides interpreter services.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply escalation procedures', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
    ],
    requiredForCompletion: true,
  },
  { pageId: 'M06-P12', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', pageType: 'final-assessment', title: 'Final Assessment: Communication Barriers',
    narrationText: `Twelve questions. Eighty percent required.`, contentHtml: `<h3>Final Assessment</h3><p>12 questions ┬╖ 80%</p>`,
    media: createMedia('Assessment.', '10-second 720p: Score.', 'image', 'beforeNarration', 'Assessment.'),
    duration: createDuration('ACHC-ART-M06', 'M06-P12', 'final-assessment', 'Final Assessment', `Twelve questions.`, 15),
    assessmentQuestions: [
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q01', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Successful communication requires knowing what barriers to communication exist and how to navigate around those roadblocks. These may include:', options: [createOption('a', 'Physical barriers', false, 'Physical is one type.'), createOption('b', 'Language barriers', false, 'Language is one type.'), createOption('c', 'Gender barriers', false, 'Gender is one type.'), createOption('d', 'Any of the above', true, 'Correct. Physical, language, and gender barriers are all communication barriers.')], mappedObjective: 'Identify barrier types', source: 'provided', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q02', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Non-verbal communication components can include physical appearance.', options: [createOption('a', 'True', true, 'Correct. Physical appearance is one of the components of non-verbal communication.'), createOption('b', 'False', false, 'Physical appearance IS a component of non-verbal communication.')], mappedObjective: 'Identify non-verbal components', source: 'provided', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q03', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'The agency provides qualified interpreter services at:', options: [createOption('a', 'A fee to the patient', false, 'Interpreter services are at no charge to the patient.'), createOption('b', 'No charge to the patient', true, 'Correct. The agency provides interpreter services at no charge.'), createOption('c', 'A reduced rate', false, 'No charge.'), createOption('d', 'Only if insurance covers it', false, 'No charge regardless of insurance.')], mappedObjective: 'Identify interpreter policy', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q04', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Children and other patients should NOT be used to interpret because:', options: [createOption('a', 'They lack medical vocabulary and using them compromises confidentiality', true, 'Correct. Accuracy and confidentiality are the primary concerns.'), createOption('b', 'They are too slow', false, 'Speed is not the concern.'), createOption('c', 'They are not reliable', false, 'The specific concerns are vocabulary and confidentiality.'), createOption('d', 'It is a criminal offense', false, 'It is an agency policy and quality concern.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply interpreter requirements', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q05', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'When communicating with a deaf person, you should NOT:', options: [createOption('a', 'Maintain eye contact', false, 'You SHOULD maintain eye contact.'), createOption('b', 'Yell or exaggerate lip movements', true, 'Correct. Yelling and exaggerating distort lip movements and make speechreading harder.'), createOption('c', 'Use visual aids', false, 'You SHOULD use visual aids.'), createOption('d', 'Get their attention before speaking', false, 'You SHOULD get attention first.')], mappedObjective: 'Apply deaf communication', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q06', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Teach-back means:', options: [createOption('a', 'Teaching the patient how to do backbends', false, 'Not related.'), createOption('b', 'Asking the patient to repeat information in their own words to verify your communication was effective', true, 'Correct. Teach-back verifies your communication effectiveness.'), createOption('c', 'Giving the patient a written test', false, 'Teach-back is verbal/demonstration, not a written test.'), createOption('d', 'Reading instructions back to the patient', false, 'Teach-back has the PATIENT tell you, not the reverse.')], mappedObjective: 'Define teach-back', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q07', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'For patients with low health literacy, the best teaching approach is:', options: [createOption('a', 'Give them more written materials', false, 'More materials does not help if the patient cannot read them.'), createOption('b', 'Use demonstrations, visual aids, simple language, and teach-back', true, 'Correct. Multiple modalities adapted to the patient\'s level.'), createOption('c', 'Speak very slowly and loudly', false, 'Low literacy is not a hearing problem.'), createOption('d', 'Only speak to the family', false, 'The patient should be involved in their care education.')], mappedObjective: 'Adapt teaching for low literacy', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q08', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'When a patient nods but gives inconsistent answers, this suggests:', options: [createOption('a', 'The patient is lying', false, 'Inconsistency usually indicates comprehension barriers, not dishonesty.'), createOption('b', 'A communication barrier may be preventing true understanding', true, 'Correct. Nodding with inconsistent answers signals a comprehension gap that needs different communication strategies.'), createOption('c', 'The patient does not care about their health', false, 'Communication barriers do not indicate lack of caring.'), createOption('d', 'Nothing unusual', false, 'Inconsistent answers always warrant further assessment.')], mappedObjective: 'Recognize comprehension barriers', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
      { questionId: 'M06-FIN-Q09', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M06', questionText: 'Which interpreter service number is provided by the agency?', options: [createOption('a', '1-800-322-0284 (Accredited Language Services)', true, 'Correct. This is one of the agency\'s contracted interpreter services.'), createOption('b', '911', false, '911 is for emergencies, not interpretation.'), createOption('c', '1-800-321-OSHA', false, 'This is OSHA, not interpreter services.'), createOption('d', '411', false, '411 is directory assistance.')], mappedObjective: 'Identify interpreter resources', source: 'supplemental', reviewRequired: false },
