  pages: [
    { pageId: 'M09-P01', moduleId: 'ACHC-ART-M09', pageType: 'overview', title: 'Corporate Compliance ΓÇö Overview',
      narrationText: `Welcome to Module Nine: Corporate Compliance. A corporate compliance program is a system designed to detect and prevent violations of law by agents, employees, officers, and directors of a business. In 1991, the federal government enacted the Organizational Sentencing Guidelines to make penalties for corporate crime uniform and predictable, encouraging good corporate citizenship. Penalties include fines, imprisonment, restitution, sanctions, forfeiture, and corporate probation. An organization with an effective compliance program may receive substantially reduced fines and may avoid probation and prosecution. Your agency's compliance plan addresses written policies and standards of conduct, designation of a Compliance Officer and Committee, training and education, open communication lines with non-retaliation protection, internal auditing, disciplinary guidelines, and prompt corrective action. The Administrator serves as the Corporate Compliance Officer. The Board of Directors has ultimate responsibility. Every employee from the CEO to the home health aide must comply. Employees can report fraud, waste, and abuse to the OIG and are protected under the Whistleblower Protection Act. This module covers the compliance program structure, fraud and abuse prevention, the False Claims Act, billing integrity, exclusion screening, conflicts of interest, reporting channels, non-retaliation, and your individual obligations. Eighty percent passing required.`,
      contentHtml: `<h2>Module 9: Corporate Compliance</h2><p>Compliance program structure, fraud/waste/abuse, False Claims, billing, exclusions, conflicts, reporting, non-retaliation.</p>`,
