        content: `<h2>Infection Prevention ΓÇö CL-SD-016</h2>
<p>Infection prevention is a <strong>core CMS requirement</strong> under 42 CFR ┬º 484.70. Understanding how infections spread is the foundation of preventing them.</p>
<div style="background:#E3F2FD;padding:16px;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0;">
<h3>The Chain of Infection ΓÇö All 6 links must be present for infection to occur:</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Infectious Agent</strong> ΓÇö the pathogen (bacteria, virus, fungus)</li>
<li><strong>Reservoir</strong> ΓÇö where the pathogen lives (human, animal, surface)</li>
<li><strong>Portal of Exit</strong> ΓÇö how it leaves the reservoir (blood, secretions, respiratory)</li>
<li><strong>Mode of Transmission</strong> ΓÇö how it travels (contact, droplet, airborne)</li>
<li><strong>Portal of Entry</strong> ΓÇö how it enters a new host (wound, mucous membrane, respiratory)</li>
<li><strong>Susceptible Host</strong> ΓÇö a person whose immune system cannot fight it off</li>
</ol>
</div>
<p><strong>Break ANY link = prevent infection.</strong> Your primary tools are hand hygiene, PPE, and standard precautions.</p>`,
